/*
========================================================
        withContext() — COMPLETE MASTER SUMMARY
========================================================

DEFINITION
--------------------------------------------------------
withContext() is a suspending function that:

✔ Switches coroutine context (like Dispatcher)
✔ Does NOT create a new coroutine
✔ Keeps the same Job (structured concurrency)
✔ Returns a result
✔ May or may NOT switch physical thread

========================================================
YOUR TEST CASES EXPLAINED (VERY IMPORTANT)
========================================================

--------------------------------------------------------
TEST CASE 1
--------------------------------------------------------

GlobalScope.launch {                 // Uses Dispatchers.Default
    withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
    }
}

Flow:

Default → IO

Why thread did NOT change?

✔ Dispatchers.Default and Dispatchers.IO
  share the SAME CoroutineScheduler
✔ Same worker thread reused
✔ Context changed logically
✔ Physical thread reuse happened for performance

So:

Context switched ✅
Thread switched ❌ (not required)


--------------------------------------------------------
TEST CASE 2
--------------------------------------------------------

GlobalScope.launch(Dispatchers.Main) {
    withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
    }
}

Flow:

Main → IO

Why thread changed?

✔ Main dispatcher uses UI thread
✔ IO dispatcher uses background worker pool
✔ Different thread pools
✔ Switching is REQUIRED
✔ Scheduler moved coroutine to worker thread

So:

Context switched ✅
Thread switched ✅ (mandatory due to different pools)


========================================================
WHAT withContext() CHANGES
========================================================

1) Dispatcher  ✅ (If Provided)

   withContext(Dispatchers.IO) { }

   ✔ Execution responsibility moves to IO dispatcher
   ✔ Scheduler decides which thread to use


2) CoroutineName  ✅ (If Provided)

   withContext(CoroutineName("MyTask")) { }

   ✔ Useful for debugging


3) CoroutineExceptionHandler  ✅ (If Provided)

   withContext(myExceptionHandler) { }

   ✔ Overrides handler inside this block


========================================================
WHAT withContext() DOES NOT CHANGE
========================================================

1) Job  ❌ (Does NOT change by default)

   ✔ Same Job is preserved
   ✔ Structured concurrency maintained
   ✔ Cancellation propagates properly
   ✔ Not a new coroutine


2) It does NOT create a new coroutine  ❌

   launch { }  → Creates new coroutine
   async { }   → Creates new coroutine
   withContext { } → Suspends current coroutine


3) Thread is NOT guaranteed to change  ❌

   Dispatcher switch ≠ Thread switch

   Thread changes only if:
   - Target dispatcher uses different thread pool
   - Scheduler decides to move it


========================================================
WHEN SHOULD YOU USE withContext() ?
========================================================

1) Move heavy work off Main thread (MOST COMMON)

   lifecycleScope.launch {

       val data = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
           apiCall()
       }

       textView.text = data
   }


2) CPU Intensive Work

   withContext(Dispatchers.Default) {
       heavyCalculation()
   }


3) When you need result sequentially

   val result = withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
       fetchData()
   }


========================================================
WHEN NOT TO USE withContext()
========================================================

❌ If already on same dispatcher
❌ If you don't need result (use launch instead)
❌ Do NOT use GlobalScope in Android (use lifecycleScope)


========================================================
INTERNAL EXECUTION FLOW
========================================================

1) Suspend current coroutine
2) Combine CurrentContext + NewContext
3) Dispatcher schedules execution
4) Execute block
5) Resume back to original context


========================================================
CORE MENTAL MODEL
========================================================

withContext changes COROUTINE CONTEXT.
Thread switching is only an implementation detail
of the dispatcher.

Context switch ALWAYS happens.
Thread switch happens ONLY when required.


========================================================
INTERVIEW ONE-LINER
========================================================

withContext switches coroutine context (dispatcher,
name, exception handler if provided), keeps the same Job,
does not create a new coroutine, and thread switching
depends on whether the target dispatcher uses a different
thread pool.
========================================================

2 main execution environments:
- UI Thread (Main)
- CoroutineScheduler thread pool (Default + IO)

✅ Main Dispatchers in Android Kotlin

In Android (kotlinx.coroutines), you mainly have:

Dispatchers.Main
Dispatchers.Default
Dispatchers.IO
Dispatchers.Unconfined


                Android App

                ┌─────────────┐
                │   Main      │
                │ (UI Thread) │
                └─────────────┘
                        ↑
                        │
      -----------------------------------------
      |        CoroutineScheduler Pool       |
      |                                       |
      |   Default Threads (CPU limited)       |
      |   IO Threads (can expand up to 64+)   |
      -----------------------------------------
